<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Khadas Edge1 (RK3399) - Android TV 14 device VINTF manifest.

  Deliberately minimal.

  In Android 14 almost every HAL service installs its own VINTF fragment next to
  its binary (Soong's vintf_fragments:), and those fragments are merged into this
  manifest at build time. The module list of a synced android-14.0.0_r75 tree
  confirms it for the services this device uses - allocator.minigbm.xml,
  mapper.minigbm.xml and android.hardware.audio.service-aidl.xml are all real
  installed artefacts. Declaring the same HAL here as well duplicates it.

  So this file declares only what no fragment covers, and everything else is left
  to the services themselves. If check_vintf reports a HAL the framework requires
  and nothing declares, add it here - that error names the interface exactly,
  which is a better source of truth than guessing up front.

  Earlier revisions of this file declared 22 AIDL HALs. All 22 were verified to
  exist at the declared versions (build/verify-aidl-surface.sh against a real
  tree), so nothing here was wrong - it was redundant, which fails differently.

  target-level="7", not "8", and that is the one number in this file worth
  explaining.

  8 is the Framework Compatibility Matrix level for Android 14, and it was set here
  first. check_vintf rejected it:

    The following instances are in the device manifest but not specified in
    framework compatibility matrix:
        android.hardware.cas@1.2::IMediaCasService/default
        android.hardware.graphics.composer@2.4::IComposer/default

  Both HALs are real and both are provided. What changed at level 8 is that the
  framework stopped listing their HIDL versions. Read out of the matrices in the
  synced tree rather than recalled:

    level 7   android.hardware.cas                hidl 1.1-2
              android.hardware.graphics.composer  hidl 2.1-4
              android.hardware.graphics.composer3 aidl 1
    level 8   android.hardware.cas                aidl only
              android.hardware.graphics.composer3 aidl 2

  So level 7 is the highest level at which this vendor image is describable, and
  that is an honest description of it rather than a workaround: the composer is
  HIDL because AOSP 14's drm_hwcomposer snapshot is HWC2 only, and the HIDL cas
  service is installed by AOSP itself - base_vendor.mk puts
  android.hardware.cas@1.2-service in PRODUCT_PACKAGES_SHIPPING_API_LEVEL_33, and
  this device ships at API 29. AOSP's own configuration assumes a device like this
  one sits below level 8.

  Lowering is permitted because this is an upgrade device, not a launch device. An
  upgrade device keeps the FCM level it launched with and may raise it; API 29 is
  level 4, so 7 is already a raise. The floor in practice is 5, which is the oldest
  matrix AOSP 14 still installs.
-->
<manifest version="7.0" type="device" target-level="7">

    <!-- ===================================================================
         Display.

         HIDL composer@2.4, not AIDL composer3, and that is a considered choice.

         The AIDL composer3 implementation for this board does not exist: writing
         one means implementing IComposerClient's 48 methods. What AOSP 14 does
         ship is drm_hwcomposer, a generic atomic-KMS composer that works with the
         Rockchip DRM driver - but the AOSP 14 snapshot of it is HWC2 only. Its
         tree contains hwc2_device/ and not a single reference to composer3.

         HWC2 is reached through the HIDL passthrough service
         android.hardware.graphics.composer@2.4-service, which dlopens
         hwcomposer.$(ro.hardware.hwcomposer).so - here hwcomposer.drm_minigbm.
         That service does not ship a fragment, so it is declared here.

         If check_vintf rejects HIDL composer at FCM level 8, the options are to
         lower target-level or to write the composer3 service. Lowering is
         defensible for an upgrade device whose shipping API level is 29.
         =================================================================== -->
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.graphics.composer</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>2.4</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IComposer</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>

    <!-- ===================================================================
         No DRM entry.

         This declared HIDL android.hardware.drm@4.0 with clearkey instances, and
         that service is not what the tree installs: device.mk installs
         android.hardware.drm-service.clearkey, the AIDL one, which ships its own
         VINTF fragment and declares itself. The HIDL @4.0 clearkey service does
         not exist in AOSP 14.

         Nothing at build time catches a manifest entry for a service that was
         never installed - check_vintf only asks whether the framework's
         requirements are met, not whether our own promises are kept - so this
         would have surfaced as the media framework looking for a hwbinder DRM HAL
         that never registers. verify-tree.sh check 4b now cross-checks each entry
         against PRODUCT_PACKAGES.

         Widevine stays absent for the original reason: drm-service.widevine comes
         from vendor/widevine, which this overlay does not sync.
         =================================================================== -->

    <!-- No <sepolicy> block. It said 29.0 here, to match
         PRODUCT_SHIPPING_API_LEVEL, and assemble_vintf refused:

           Cannot override existing value 29.0 with BOARD_SEPOLICY_VERS
           (which is 202404).

         The build passes BOARD_SEPOLICY_VERS into assemble_vintf as an
         environment override and will not overwrite a value the input manifest
         already carries. 202404 is right and 29.0 was not: core/config.mk derives
         BOARD_SEPOLICY_VERS from PLATFORM_SEPOLICY_VERSION, which is the version
         of the policy this vendor code is actually compiled against, not the
         shipping API level. So the field is left for the build to fill, the same
         way BOARD_SEPOLICY_VERS itself is (see BoardConfig.mk, where setting it by
         hand was also removed).
    -->
</manifest>
